{1}{3}$ | |  | | $y$ | $-2$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $\\frac{3}{8}$ | $\\frac{5}{8}$ | |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $3$ |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| $y$ | $-2$ | $2$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $\\frac{3}{8}$ | $\\frac{5}{8}$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P(X=0,Y=-2)=P(X=0)\\cdot P(Y=-2)$\n- $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n- $X$ and $Y$ are independent" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14085343/state.json" "file_sha256" "f77d0929ea499c34b088d112cf3d58bc4981ae3ea6d7904d61b0e553793ea717"} {"question" "q-163605" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=0,Y=-2)=f(0,-2)=\\frac{1}{4}.$ On the other hand, \n\n$$\nP(X=0)\\cdot P(Y=-2)={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(-2)=\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{8}=\\frac{1}{4}.\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Checking all other possible products, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,2) & =\\frac{5}{12}=\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{5}{8}={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(2) & ✓ \\\\ f(3,-2) & =\\frac{1}{8}=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{8}={f}_{X}(3)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(-2) & ✓ \\\\ f(3,2) & =\\frac{5}{24}=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{5}{8}={f}_{X}(3)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(2) & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ must be independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "