th a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nComputing the marginal probability mass functions for  $X$ and $Y,$ we obtain the following:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.6$ | $0.2$ | $0.8$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.15$ | $0.05$ | $0.2$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.75$ | $0.25$ | $1$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=2,Y=1)=f(2,1)=0.15.$ On the other hand, \n\n$$\nP(X=2)\\cdot P(Y=1)={f}_{X}(2)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1)=0.2\\cdot 0.75=0.15.\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Checking all other possible products, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1) & =0.6=0.8\\cdot 0.75={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1) & ✓ \\\\ f(1,2) & =0.2=0.8\\cdot 0.25={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(2) & ✓ \\\\ f(2,2) & =0.05=0.2\\cdot 0.25={f}_{X}(2)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(2) & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ must be independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14085343/state.json" "file_sha256" "f77d0929ea499c34b088d112cf3d58bc4981ae3ea6d7904d61b0e553793ea717"} {"question" "q-143331" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14085343/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3c0ad2d740ee1300d6bd9d08461759b3f3c0a1b5010a1f8d0fc839acd4f1e30c"} {"question" "q-143331" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "ca