 in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=3,Y=2)=f(3,2)=0.64.$ On the other hand, \n\n$$\nP(X=3)\\cdot P(Y=2)={f}_{X}(3)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(2)=0.8\\cdot 0.8=0.64.\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Checking some possible products, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,2) & =0.16=0.2\\cdot 0.8={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(2) & ✓ \\\\ f(1,4) & =0.01\\ne 0.2\\cdot 0.1={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(4) & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $P(X=x,Y=y)\\ne P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are *not* independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14085343/state.json" "file_sha256" "f77d0929ea499c34b088d112cf3d58bc4981ae3ea6d7904d61b0e553793ea717"} {"question" "q-143338" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14085343/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3c0ad2d740ee1300d6bd9d08461759b3f3c0a1b5010a1f8d0fc839acd4f1e30c"} {"question" "q-143338" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14085343/state.json" "file_sha256" "f77d0929ea499c34b088d112cf3d58bc4981ae3ea6d7904d61b0e553793ea717"} {"question" "q-143338" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None of those listed"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14085343/state.json" "file_sha256" "f77d0929ea499c34b088d112cf3d58bc4981ae3ea6d7904d61b0e55379